{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 1,
  "simulation_id": "sim-05",
  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-6-luna",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_luna_round1_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B027",
        "laureates": [
          "Alexandre Blais",
          "Andreas Wallraff",
          "Robert J. Schoelkopf"
        ],
        "citation": "For establishing circuit quantum electrodynamics with superconducting artificial atoms."
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B004",
    "B050"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "Circuit QED made a new regime of light–matter interaction experimentally accessible: microwave photons in engineered circuits could be coupled strongly to artificial atoms, controlled, and measured in ways that enabled precise tests of quantum optics and a durable platform for quantum information. The theory and experiments form a coherent, prize-sized advance, supported by decades of reproducible work.",
    "case_against": "The achievement grew from foundational theory and many experimental and engineering contributions, making the boundary around three laureates difficult; Steven M. Girvin’s foundational role is especially important to assess. The committee would also need to distinguish this advance from the 2025 prize for foundational superconducting-circuit phenomena.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "You cautioned that the Aharonov–Bohm effect and Berry phase are distinct contributions grouped under a broad theme, and that this could obscure their separate histories.",
        "response": "I agree that the combination needs a precise citation and separate attribution for each contribution. The breadth of their influence is compelling, but it makes the single-prize scope less clean than circuit QED’s focused achievement of controlling light–matter interaction in a circuit."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "You argued that optical lattice clocks are exceptionally mature, reproducible across laboratories, and valuable for precision tests, while noting that the clock’s early concepts and later contributions need careful separation.",
        "response": "That maturity and experimental reach make B050 a strong alternative. My reservation is that its case centers on a precision instrument and the boundary between parallel concepts and later advances. Circuit QED more directly established a new, controllable regime of quantum physics, though the distinction from the 2025 prize must be resolved."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "How to define circuit QED’s distinct contribution relative to the 2025 prize on superconducting-circuit phenomena.",
      "Whether the proposed three names fairly capture the theoretical and experimental foundations, particularly Girvin’s role."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "My WACQT directorship and professional work in superconducting quantum circuits create a possible field-proximity conflict for B027. The supplied profile shows no direct personal or coauthorship connection to the three proposed laureates; whether formal recusal is required is unclear."
  }
}
